+## TL;DR
+- **Best all-around private AI workspace:** [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui) for local or self-hosted chat, RAG, permissions, and voice features.
+- **Best document Q&A stacks:** [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), [Kotaemon](https://github.com/Cinnamon/kotaemon), and [Open Deep Research](https://github.com/langchain-ai/open_deep_research).
+- **Best workflow builders for agency ops:** [n8n](https://github.com/n8n-io/n8n), [Activepieces](https://github.com/activepieces/activepieces), and [ToolJet](https://github.com/ToolJet/ToolJet).
+- **Best document intake and OCR tooling:** [PaddleOCR](https://github.com/PaddlePaddle/PaddleOCR), [Docling](https://github.com/docling-project/docling), [Tesseract](https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tesseract), and [OCRmyPDF](https://github.com/OCRmyPDF/OCRmyPDF).
+- **Best call and meeting stack:** [Whisper](https://github.com/openai/whisper), [faster-whisper](https://github.com/SYSTRAN/faster-whisper), [WhisperX](https://github.com/m-bain/whisperX), [pyannote-audio](https://github.com/pyannote/pyannote-audio), and [Cal.com](https://github.com/calcom/cal.com).
+
+## Why This List
+Insurance agencies handle sensitive documents, repetitive intake work, heavy follow-up, and a constant need to summarize complex policy information quickly. A good list of the best AI tools for insurance agents in 2026 should therefore prioritize privacy, self-hosting, extensibility, and verifiable GitHub activity instead of glossy SaaS landing pages. This repository favors tools with public source, visible licenses, active repositories, and practical fit for quoting, renewals, claims support, CRM enrichment, internal knowledge search, and customer communications.
