+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best AI tools for insurance agents in 2026 that are actually open source?
+The strongest starting points in this repo are Open WebUI, AnythingLLM, Kotaemon, PaddleOCR, Docling, Whisper, Activepieces, Chatwoot, and Cal.com. They all have public GitHub repositories and are useful in real agency workflows.
+
+### Which open source AI tools help insurance agents process PDFs and scanned documents?
+PaddleOCR, Tesseract, OCRmyPDF, Docling, Unstructured, and Marker are the best fits here. They cover OCR, searchable PDFs, layout-aware parsing, and structured extraction for downstream AI workflows.
+
+### What is the best self-hosted AI chatbot stack for an insurance agency?
+For internal document-backed chat, Open WebUI, AnythingLLM, and Kotaemon are the best options in this list. For customer-facing flows, Typebot, Rasa, and Chatwoot are more relevant.
+
+### Which GitHub tools are best for insurance lead capture and website chat?
+Typebot, Chatwoot, Papercups, Formbricks, and Cal.com are the clearest matches. Together they cover chat, forms, surveys, booking, and structured intake.
+
+### Can insurance agencies build private AI assistants without using proprietary SaaS?
+Yes. A practical self-hosted stack could combine Open WebUI or AnythingLLM for RAG, PaddleOCR or Docling for document prep, Whisper or faster-whisper for calls, and Activepieces or n8n for workflow automation.
+
+### What is the best open source voice transcription setup for insurance calls?
+A common stack is Whisper or faster-whisper for transcription, WhisperX for word-level timestamps, and pyannote-audio for diarization. That gives you transcripts plus speaker-aware structure for QA and summaries.
+
+### Why are there fewer voice repos in this list than document or workflow repos?
+Because the public GitHub market is genuinely thinner for insurance-relevant voice tooling once you exclude closed SaaS and weak fits. This repository documents that gap honestly instead of padding the list.
+
